    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d729">

                <pb facs="1435" n="1423" xml:id="pg1423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.50/2–2845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-28T10:00:00+03:00">February 28,
                            1945—10 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 28—7:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>572. ReDeptel 359, February 17, midnight.<anchor n="53" xml:id="d729-f1"/> I have
                    received a note from Vyshinski<anchor n="54" xml:id="d729-f2"/> dated February
                    26, which states that the Soviet Government agrees to initiate conversations of
                    an exploratory character with representatives of the Governments of the US,
                    Great Britain and France on the creation of a European Economic Committee and a
                    European Coal Organization and to discuss the questions set forth in the amended
                    terms of reference as contained in Balfour’s<anchor n="55" xml:id="d729-f3"/>
                    letter of January 21 (reEmbs 223, January 24, 4 p.m.<anchor n="56"
                        xml:id="d729-f4"/>).</p>
                <p>The note continues that the Soviet Government, considering a discussion of these
                    questions important, does not object to the proposed conference in London
                    between Soviet, British, American and French representatives for the purpose of
                    creating a European Economic Committee and a European Coal Organization. To
                    participate in these conversations the Soviet Government has appointed as its
                    representatives the trade representative of the USSR in London, D. B. Borisenko,
                    and Professor Bayar.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d729-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent to the Department repeated to London.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
